The `.expected` was blessed against macOS ruby, and the last line's value
is a libm result: `(-8.0) ** (1.0/3)` is a complex power, so the answer
comes from the platform's `pow`/`exp`/`log`.

macOS prints  (1.0+1.7320508075688772i)
Linux prints  (1.0000000000000002+1.7320508075688772i)

Confirmed by running CRuby itself on Linux (aarch64-linux-gnu): it prints
the second, which is exactly what zeo prints there. So Linux CRuby
diverges from the committed macOS-oracle expectation in the same way and
by the same one ULP that zeo does -- there is nothing here for zeo to fix,
and pinning the macOS value on both platforms would only make the suite
lie on one of them.
